    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `arrange`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `arrange` function is responsible for organizing and positioning a collection of widgets within a given size and viewport. It takes four parameters: `widget`, which is the parent widget; `children`, a sequence of child widgets to be arranged; `size`, the total size available for arrangement; and `viewport`, the visible area within the size. The function returns a `DockArrangeResult` object containing the arranged widget placements, a set of displayed widgets, and scroll spacing.\n\nThe function first filters out any child widgets that are not meant to be displayed based on their styles. It then organizes the remaining widgets into layers and processes each layer separately. Within each layer, it partitions widgets into split and non-split groups. Split widgets are arranged using the `_arrange_split_widgets` helper function, while non-split widgets are further divided into layout and dock widgets. Dock widgets are positioned relative to the edges using `_arrange_dock_widgets`, and layout widgets are arranged according to the parent widget's layout rules.\n\nThe function handles alignment of layout widgets based on the parent widget's styles and adjusts their positions if necessary. It also calculates scroll spacing to accommodate any overflow. The function does not handle errors or edge cases explicitly; instead, it relies on the helper functions to manage such scenarios. There are no side effects; the function only returns the arrangement results and does not modify any external state.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
